  "notes": {
   "forAnalyst": "Lets the business subscribe an external system to things that happen in the store — an order placed, a price changed — without a developer writing an integration for each one. Someone configures the endpoint and the fields to send in the back office, and every delivery attempt is auditable afterwards.",
   "forArchitect": "The platform's only built-in outbound integration edge. It turns in-process domain events into HTTP POSTs, which means it is the boundary where a Virto event stops being transactional: delivery is a background job with retries, not part of the writing transaction. It declares no module dependencies, so it can be installed on any host — but it needs a background-jobs worker to deliver anything, and it owns four tables of its own.",
   "forDeveloper": "You rarely code against it. Raise a proper domain event in your module and it becomes subscribable automatically, because the module resolves DomainEvent types from every loaded assembly at runtime. The two knobs that change behaviour are settings, not code: Webhooks.General.SendRetryCount and Webhooks.General.LatestErrorCount.",
   "owns": [
    "WebhookEntity",
    "WebhookEventEntity",
    "WebHookPayloadEntity",
    "WebhookFeedEntryEntity"
   ],
   "reachForItWhen": [
    "An external system needs to react to store changes and you do not want it polling your API",
    "Integration middleware wants a change stream rather than a nightly diff",
    "A partner asked for callbacks and you need the delivery attempts on record"
   ],
   "doNotReachForItWhen": [
    "The reaction belongs inside the platform — that is a domain event handler, in-process and transactional",
    "You need guaranteed, ordered delivery: this is retried HTTP with exponential backoff, not a broker. Reach for EventBus and a real queue",
    "The payload would carry data the receiving endpoint should not see — field selection is per webhook and easy to over-share",
    "The events come from a bulk import: suppress events or turn event-based indexation off first, or one import fans out into thousands of callbacks"
   ]
  },
